{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat source_evidence/downstream/program/src/IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md; sed -n '1,280p' source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py; sed -n '1,220p' source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py; ls -la task; ls task/; rg --files task/ | head -50; sed -n '1,100p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat source_evidence/downstream/program/src/IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md; sed -n '1,280p' source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py; sed -n '1,220p' source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py; ls -la task; ls task/; rg --files task/ | head -50; sed -n '1,100p' .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Budget-matched coalition design\n\n## Implemented scope\n\nThe entrypoint remains `program.py:handle`. Its only responses are the original\n`{\"ok\": true}`, `{\"masks\": ...}`, and `{\"phi\": ...}`. Reset erases the live\nexplanation; start creates or replaces it; observe consumes it. Seed and stream\ninitialize all randomness. Input/background fields remain opaque: the supplied\ninterface owns masking, fitted preprocessing, and probability evaluation.\n\nThe candidate changes the coalition design, not the estimand or regression.\nEmpty and full coalitions remain first and second. Exactly the requested number\nof rows is returned, including duplicate rows and endpoints; no hidden model\ncalls or extra observations are used. Observe uses the starter's identical\nefficiency-constrained, equal-weight least-squares solve and tolerance.\n\n## Sampling method\n\nFor dimension d, combine sizes s and d-s into an unordered complement stratum.\nIts probability is proportional to 2/[s(d-s)], except the central size in even\ndimensions, whose weight is 1/[s(d-s)]. Randomized equal-probability strata of\nthe cumulative distribution reduce variation in size counts. Randomly permute\nthe selected sizes and choose uniform subsets within each size. Remember chosen\nunordered complement pairs until that size's finite population is exhausted,\nthen start another cycle. Random orientation restores the original distribution\nover individual masks. Each mask has the original kernel marginal distribution;\nthe masks are intentionally not independent within a run.\n\nComplementary observations constrain the odd part of the coalition response\ntogether and can reduce Monte Carlo variability. However, their regression\ndesign rows are negatives of one another, so they do not provide two independent\ndesign directions. If N is the interior row budget, use at most\nmin(floor(N/2), max(0, N-2(d-1))) pairs. The remaining rows are individually\nsampled from the same stratified distribution. Thus pairing alone cannot reduce\nthe number of potentially distinct directions below min(N, 2(d-1)). This is\nrank headroom, not a guarantee of full rank; the original least-squares rule\nstill handles singular designs. All mask generation is local to start, with\nno cross-explanation cache of coalition values or other run observations.\n\nThis design is a variance-reduction hypothesis, not an accuracy guarantee.\nKernel marginals alone do not imply unbiasedness of a finite-sample regression\ncoefficient, nor universal improvement from pairing. Neither repeat agreement\nnor exact efficiency establishes attribution accuracy or causal validity.\n\n## Interface limitations and unperformed work\n\nThe interface does not expose a prediction callable, dataset identifiers,\ntraining controls, original row identifiers, timing of black-box evaluation,\nor independently queryable reference values. Consequently this candidate cannot\nimplement the proposed fixed-model Pima/ACS exact-reference study, German Credit\nreference ensembles, prediction-row cap calibration, package-based SHAP calls,\nbackground-resampling experiments, clustered reliability bootstrap, or empirical\npaper. The background length is visible, but is not evidence of how many model\nrows the external implementation actually evaluated. No such measurements or\nartifacts are fabricated, and no diagnostic keys are added to the response.\n\nNo experiments, tests, literature searches, package installations, or evaluations\nwere run during this implementation stage. In particular there are no measured\nreliability claims, performance results, verified official conference templates,\nor completed paper deliverables. Only static source inspection was authorized.\n\"\"\"Finite-budget interventional Kernel SHAP, CPU/NumPy, handle-only API.\n\nAll requests/responses are finite JSON objects. Extra response keys are forbidden.\nSeeds are arbitrary nonnegative integers and initialize randomness, never workload.\nEvery episode starts with reset; subsequent independent explanations use start.\n\nreset: {\"op\":\"reset\",\"seed\":int} -> {\"ok\":true}. Erases all state.\nstart: {\"op\":\"start\", \"features\":int in [2,20], \"budget\":int in [32,256],\n        \"stream\":nonnegative int, \"input\":list[features],\n        \"background\":list[1..32][features], \"output\":\"positive_probability\",\n        \"value_function\":\"interventional_grouped\"}\n  Original columns are the players; numeric/string category values are opaque.\n  The evaluator masks complete original columns before training-fitted encoding.\n  Returns {\"masks\":list[budget][features]} of integer 0/1 values, first empty,\n  second full. All interior masks must be nonempty/nonfull. One live explanation;\n  start may discard prior state after a failure. No paths or model labels exist.\nobserve: {\"op\":\"observe\",\"values\":list[budget] of finite probabilities}\n  Supplies the background-averaged black-box model values in mask order.\n  Returns {\"phi\":list[features] of finite numbers, abs(value)<=1e6} on the\n  probability scale. Strings and booleans are not attribution numbers.\n  Consumes the live explanation. Solve a Shapley-kernel sampled constrained\n  regression: sampling a size proportional to 1/(size*(d-size)), then a uniform\n  subset, makes the interior least-squares weights equal. This implementation\n  stratifies that distribution, pairs complements when the design has enough\n  rows, and covers each size stratum without replacement before recycling.\n  Samples are dependent within a run, with the same kernel marginals.\n  Empty/full endpoints\n  impose exact efficiency (lstsq rcond=1e-10). Regression auditing uses\n  rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-7. The evaluator checks masks, budgets, the regression,\n  synthetic exact controls and reset replay, not candidate internal claims.\n\nBudgets include endpoints, duplicates and all model evaluations. No optimization\nor metric feedback is delivered. Independent repetitions have distinct stream\nIDs; same reset/stream/settings must replay deterministically. No training,\nstate export, file exchange, or large-value transport is needed: all bounded\nmessages fit below 64 MiB including JSON/envelope/framing. Larger shapes are\ninvalid, not requests to load files. Each run has exactly start + observe RPCs.\nSearch ranks evidence completion, not a favorable reliability conclusion. The\npaper still needs real results, uncertainty, references, official conference\ntemplate/checklist and all reproduction code; this starter is not a paper.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom math import comb\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n_seed = 0\n_active = None\n\n\ndef _stratified_sizes(feature_count, count, generator):\n    \"\"\"Sample unordered complement strata with kernel-proportional marginals.\"\"\"\n    if count == 0:\n        return np.empty(0, dtype=np.int64)\n    sizes = np.arange(1, feature_count // 2 + 1, dtype=np.int64)\n    weights = 1.0 / (sizes * (feature_count - sizes))\n    weights *= np.where(2 * sizes == feature_count, 1.0, 2.0)\n    cumulative = np.cumsum(weights / weights.sum())\n    cumulative[-1] = 1.0\n    positions = (np.arange(count) + generator.random(count)) / count\n    selected = sizes[np.searchsorted(cumulative, positions, side=\"right\")]\n    generator.shuffle(selected)\n    return selected\n\n\ndef _draw_mask(feature_count, size, generator, visited):\n    \"\"\"Draw a uniformly oriented pair, without replacement within each cycle.\"\"\"\n    full_mask = (1 << feature_count) - 1\n    capacity = comb(feature_count, size)\n    if 2 * size == feature_count:\n        capacity //= 2\n    seen = visited.setdefault(size, set())\n    if len(seen) == capacity:\n        seen.clear()\n    while True:\n        columns = generator.choice(feature_count, size=size, replace=False)\n        encoded = sum(1 << int(column_index) for column_index in columns)\n        if 2 * size == feature_count:\n            encoded = min(encoded, full_mask ^ encoded)\n        if encoded not in seen:\n            seen.add(encoded)\n            break\n    if generator.integers(2):\n        encoded = full_mask ^ encoded\n    return np.asarray(\n        [(encoded >> column_index) & 1 for column_index in range(feature_count)],\n        dtype=np.int64,\n    )\n\n\ndef _coalition_design(feature_count, budget, generator):\n    \"\"\"Spend exactly budget rows, retaining rank headroom before pairing.\"\"\"\n    masks = np.zeros((budget, feature_count), dtype=np.int64)\n    masks[1] = 1\n    interior_count = budget - 2\n    pair_count = min(\n        interior_count // 2,\n        max(0, interior_count - 2 * (feature_count - 1)),\n    )\n    single_count = interior_count - 2 * pair_count\n    pair_sizes = _stratified_sizes(feature_count, pair_count, generator)\n    single_sizes = _stratified_sizes(feature_count, single_count, generator)\n    visited = {}\n    row_index = 2\n    for size in pair_sizes:\n        mask = _draw_mask(feature_count, int(size), generator, visited)\n        masks[row_index] = mask\n        masks[row_index + 1] = 1 - mask\n        row_index += 2\n    for size in single_sizes:\n        masks[row_index] = _draw_mask(feature_count, int(size), generator, visited)\n        row_index += 1\n    generator.shuffle(masks[2:])\n    return masks\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    global _seed, _active\n    operation = request[\"op\"]\n    if operation == \"reset\":\n        if type(request[\"seed\"]) is not int or request[\"seed\"] < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid seed\")\n        _seed = request[\"seed\"]\n        _active = None\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    if operation == \"start\":\n        features = request[\"features\"]\n        budget = request[\"budget\"]\n        if not 2 <= features <= 20 or not 32 <= budget <= 256:\n            raise ValueError(\"unsupported shape\")\n        generator = np.random.default_rng(np.random.SeedSequence([_seed, request[\"stream\"]]))\n        masks = _coalition_design(features, budget, generator)\n        _active = masks\n        return {\"masks\": masks.tolist()}\n    if operation == \"observe\":\n        if _active is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"no active explanation\")\n        masks = _active\n        values = np.asarray(request[\"values\"], dtype=np.float64)\n        total = values[1] - values[0]\n        design = masks[2:, :-1] - masks[2:, -1:]\n        target = values[2:] - values[0] - masks[2:, -1] * total\n        partial = np.linalg.lstsq(design, target, rcond=1e-10)[0]\n        attribution = np.append(partial, total - partial.sum())\n        _active = None\n        return {\"phi\": attribution.tolist()}\n    raise ValueError(\"unknown operation\")\n\"\"\"Finite-budget interventional Kernel SHAP, CPU/NumPy, handle-only API.\n\nAll requests/responses are finite JSON objects. Extra response keys are forbidden.\nSeeds are arbitrary nonnegative integers and initialize randomness, never workload.\nEvery episode starts with reset; subsequent independent explanations use start.\n\nreset: {\"op\":\"reset\",\"seed\":int} -> {\"ok\":true}. Erases all state.\nstart: {\"op\":\"start\", \"features\":int in [2,20], \"budget\":int in [32,256],\n        \"stream\":nonnegative int, \"input\":list[features],\n        \"background\":list[1..32][features], \"output\":\"positive_probability\",\n        \"value_function\":\"interventional_grouped\"}\n  Original columns are the players; numeric/string category values are opaque.\n  The evaluator masks complete original columns before training-fitted encoding.\n  Returns {\"masks\":list[budget][features]} of integer 0/1 values, first empty,\n  second full. All interior masks must be nonempty/nonfull. One live explanation;\n  start may discard prior state after a failure. No paths or model labels exist.\nobserve: {\"op\":\"observe\",\"values\":list[budget] of finite probabilities}\n  Supplies the background-averaged black-box model values in mask order.\n  Returns {\"phi\":list[features] of finite numbers, abs(value)<=1e6} on the\n  probability scale. Strings and booleans are not attribution numbers.\n  Consumes the live explanation. Solve a Shapley-kernel sampled constrained\n  regression: sampling a size proportional to 1/(size*(d-size)), then a uniform\n  subset, makes the interior least-squares weights equal. Empty/full endpoints\n  impose exact efficiency (lstsq rcond=1e-10). Regression auditing uses\n  rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-7. The evaluator checks masks, budgets, the regression,\n  synthetic exact controls and reset replay, not candidate internal claims.\n\nBudgets include endpoints, duplicates and all model evaluations. No optimization\nor metric feedback is delivered. Independent repetitions have distinct stream\nIDs; same reset/stream/settings must replay deterministically. No training,\nstate export, file exchange, or large-value transport is needed: all bounded\nmessages fit below 64 MiB including JSON/envelope/framing. Larger shapes are\ninvalid, not requests to load files. Each run has exactly start + observe RPCs.\nSearch ranks evidence completion, not a favorable reliability conclusion. The\npaper still needs real results, uncertainty, references, official conference\ntemplate/checklist and all reproduction code; this starter is not a paper.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n_seed = 0\n_active = None\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    global _seed, _active\n    operation = request[\"op\"]\n    if operation == \"reset\":\n        if type(request[\"seed\"]) is not int or request[\"seed\"] < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid seed\")\n        _seed = request[\"seed\"]\n        _active = None\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    if operation == \"start\":\n        features = request[\"features\"]\n        budget = request[\"budget\"]\n        if not 2 <= features <= 20 or not 32 <= budget <= 256:\n            raise ValueError(\"unsupported shape\")\n        generator = np.random.default_rng(np.random.SeedSequence([_seed, request[\"stream\"]]))\n        sizes = np.arange(1, features)\n        probabilities = 1.0 / (sizes * (features - sizes))\n        probabilities /= probabilities.sum()\n        masks = np.zeros((budget, features), dtype=np.int64)\n        masks[1] = 1\n        for index in range(2, budget):\n            size = int(generator.choice(sizes, p=probabilities))\n            masks[index, generator.choice(features, size=size, replace=False)] = 1\n        _active = masks\n        return {\"masks\": masks.tolist()}\n    if operation == \"observe\":\n        if _active is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"no active explanation\")\n        masks = _active\n        values = np.asarray(request[\"values\"], dtype=np.float64)\n        total = values[1] - values[0]\n        design = masks[2:, :-1] - masks[2:, -1:]\n        target = values[2:] - values[0] - masks[2:, -1] * total\n        partial = np.linalg.lstsq(design, target, rcond=1e-10)[0]\n        attribution = np.append(partial, total - partial.sum())\n        _active = None\n        return {\"phi\": attribution.tolist()}\n    raise ValueError(\"unknown operation\")\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent 7 Nov 17 17:08 task -> ../task\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\nresources\ntask.md\ntask_contract.json\ntask/task.md\ntask/task_contract.json\ntask/evaluate_final.py\ntask/evaluate.py\ntask/capture.py\ntask/requirements.txt\ntask/program_0/program_spec.toml\ntask/program_0/src/program.py\ntask/resources/search/acs-dictionary.pdf\ntask/resources/search/template.zip\ntask/resources/search/german-page.html\ntask/resources/search/data.json\ntask/resources/search/manifest.json\ntask/resources/search/census-terms.html\ntask/resources/search/style.html\ntask/resources/final/acs-dictionary.pdf\ntask/resources/final/template.zip\ntask/resources/final/german-page.html\ntask/resources/final/data.json\ntask/resources/final/manifest.json\ntask/resources/final/census-terms.html\ntask/resources/final/style.html\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Budget-Matched SHAP Reliability Audits with Exact-Game Anchors. Hypothesis: fixed-game single-run reliability varies with budget and attribution separation, while repeat agreement may hide reference error. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No experiment or evaluated new mechanism supports a distilled empirical recommendation; the proposal remains an untested hypothesis.\n  - evidence: No recent ideas or attempts and empty authoritative JSONL on read. Bootstrap manifest reports combined_score=1.0 and correct=true. Named baseline source file was unavailable, leaving the candidate interface unknown. Alternatives considered: broad estimator tournament, adaptive certificate, and background-only perturbations. Selected exact-game references and measured prediction-row audits because they directly address same-model repeatability with observable candidate-owned controls. No experiments, evaluator runs, installations, or literature searches performed.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T16:36:50.070874+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 0.999999999999984, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.03279427307354245, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.928086123122648, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9629629629629629}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Kernel-Marginal Conditioning Portfolios for SHAP: eight outcome-blind candidate masks share a kernel-stratified size schedule and are selected by feature-symmetric conditioning at unchanged query budget. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=The inspected candidate contract establishes a new actionable interface-limitation category, while the recorded evaluated sampler supplies no evidence of improvement.\n  - evidence: Prior attempt: combined_score=1.0, correct=true, matched_budget_gain=-0.03279427307354245, reference_fidelity=0.928086123122648, repeat_agreement=0.9629629629629629, rubric_overall_pass=false. Accessible previous candidate source confirms reset/start/observe with mask-only control and a prescribed unweighted efficiency-constrained solver; the absolute incumbent source path is unavailable. Alternatives considered: more complementary pairing, exact dataset anchors, and outcome-adaptive allocation. Chosen conditioning portfolio is feasible without extra observations, but improved fidelity remains untested and no cause of prior underperformance is identified. No new experiment, evaluator, installation or literature search performed.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T16:44:57.478241+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 1.0, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.013125176380736481, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.8719472427342251, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9212962962962962}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Interaction-Alias-Aware Kernel Designs for SHAP: retain the eight-design shared-size portfolio but select by exact normalized monomial attribution errors rather than inverse-spectrum conditioning. gate=updated; trigger=d; reason=Qualify the previous conditioning-portfolio recommendation with its now-recorded evaluated non-improvement, without inferring a causal failure mechanism.\n  - evidence: Cycle 2: combined_score=1.0, correct=true, control_pass_fraction=1.0, coverage=1.0, matched_budget_gain=-0.013125176380736481, reference_fidelity=0.8719472427342251, repeat_agreement=0.9212962962962962; cycle 1 also lacked aggregate improvement and had negative matched-budget gain. Read named histories, campaign JSONL, conclusions and previous candidate sources. Alternative larger conditioning portfolios or complement pairing alone lack positive support; low-discrepancy priorities add a separate sampling mechanism; outcome adaptation and callable references exceed the one-batch API. Chosen mask-only alias scoring computes exact pairwise/three-way monomial regression errors at full contrast rank, but real-game improvement and CPU overhead remain unknown. No task program, evaluator, benchmark, numerical probe, package install or literature search was run.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T17:00:53.949960+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 1.0, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.010818252140058352, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.8767845417182945, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9158950617283951}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
